WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-US

00:00:01.400 --> 00:00:07.240
This domain will take us through input and output&nbsp;
operations. For the next few videos, we will work&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:07.240 --> 00:00:14.320
with files using an app's code. Regarding gaming,&nbsp;
one often needs to store values in a configuration&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:14.320 --> 00:00:22.600
or similar file for items such as player or game&nbsp;
settings. One such example is this 311-open file,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:22.600 --> 00:00:27.840
which has code that opens a file, writes to&nbsp;
it, and closes it on the first three lines&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:27.840 --> 00:00:33.800
of the code. I have that code in this file to&nbsp;
ensure we have a file to work with should you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:33.800 --> 00:00:40.360
decide to run this code from any folder. By&nbsp;
default, this code sends files to one’s user&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:40.360 --> 00:00:50.280
folder under Users. What I want to focus on is&nbsp;
the code on line 5. There is a variable called&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:50.280 --> 00:01:01.360
message test. For that variable, we see an open&nbsp;
command and the name of a file to open. The r&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:01.360 --> 00:01:09.120
argument opens the file in read mode. I will cover&nbsp;
read mode and other modes in upcoming topics. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:09.120 --> 00:01:15.280
since we are not yet reading any content from the&nbsp;
file, as we are covering that in another topic,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:15.280 --> 00:01:22.600
I have a print statement on line 6 acknowledging&nbsp;
the file is open and I will run this code to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:22.600 --> 00:01:29.920
get that acknowledgement. The key takeaway&nbsp;
here is that, when opening an external file,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:29.920 --> 00:01:35.280
we want to store that action in a variable&nbsp;
because, as we will discover in upcoming videos,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:35.280 --> 00:01:39.960
there are many methods we can run to read&nbsp;
content from a file, write to the file, or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:39.960 --> 00:01:48.400
do other actions on the file. We need to know how&nbsp;
to open a file to do those actions on the file.

